Subject: Planning objective for an 8‑node transmission buildout linking generators to consumers

Our utility is preparing to design a new transmission network that connects two generator sites to six consumer locations, eight nodes in total. We are not constrained to existing corridors; lines may be built between any pair of nodes if doing so is cost‑effective. The planning inputs are as follows:
- Node map coordinates: {NODE_COORDINATES}
- Node roles (which sites are generators vs. consumers): {NODE_TYPES}
- Consumer power requirements: {POWER_DEMANDS}
- Generator maximum outputs: {GENERATOR_CAPACITIES}
- Copper conductor parameters: resistivity {WIRE_RESISTIVITY} and cross‑section {WIRE_CROSS_SECTION}
- Nominal system voltage: {OPERATING_VOLTAGE}
- Per‑line transfer rating (applies in either direction): {LINE_CAPACITY}
- Line construction cost per kilometer: {BUILD_COST_PER_KM}
- Monetary rate applied to resistive energy losses: {LOSS_COST_RATE}

Design freedoms and obligations
- We may choose to build transmission lines between any two nodes; power can only flow where a line is actually built.
- Each built line is bidirectional; the same thermal rating limits how much power can flow in either direction at any moment.
- Every consumer must be served at or above its demand. Generator outputs cannot exceed their maximum capabilities. Serving more than the stated demand at a consumer is acceptable.
- For planning purposes, power flow is conserved at the nodes. We do not deduct physical losses from nodal balances; instead, we account for the losses as a separate operating cost.

Cost drivers and loss physics
- Capital cost scales with route length. Distances come from the map coordinates, and each kilometer of built line carries the stated construction cost. This pushes the design to avoid overly long routes when alternatives exist.
- Operationally, the dominant penalty is resistive heating in the conductors. Line resistance increases with length and decreases with cross‑section, based on the copper material properties we’ve specified. Practically, this means each additional kilometer adds resistance, and smaller conductors run hotter for a given flow.
- Losses follow a square law with respect to power flow. Because current is power divided by voltage, the physical relationship can be summarized as: resistive loss is proportional to line resistance times (power flow divided by voltage) squared. In short, Loss ~ Resistance × (Power/Voltage)^2. Doubling the power on a line increases the heating loss by roughly a factor of four.
- Operating at higher voltage directly reduces current for a given power transfer and therefore reduces heating losses by roughly 1/Voltage^2. This benefit is material at the flows we expect and should be captured precisely.
- It is important that we do not approximate these losses as linear. The square‑law behavior must be used, otherwise we will under‑ or over‑estimate the true cost of heavily loaded paths.

Planning objective
- Determine which lines to build and how to route power so that every consumer’s demand is satisfied and every generator’s limit is respected, while each line’s bidirectional transfer does not exceed its rating.
- Minimize total lifecycle cost as the sum of:
  1) construction costs driven by built line lengths, and
  2) ongoing “heating costs” arising from resistive losses on each energized line, monetized using the stated rate.
- Because losses do not reduce the nodal power balance in this planning model, the optimization must still pay for the energy dissipated as heat, which creates a real operating expense even if the delivered power remains unchanged.

In practical terms, the solution will balance three levers: shorter and fewer routes to reduce capital spend, adequate capacity on chosen lines to meet peak transfers without violating ratings, and high‑voltage operation to suppress I^2R losses—mindful that pushing more power through any single path quickly drives up the quadratic heating cost. The recommended network should reflect these physics and cost interactions so we avoid designs that look inexpensive on paper but incur outsized losses once energized.
